      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of this view often point to the \"Flynn Effect,\" arguing that when countries achieve similar levels of socioeconomic equality and education, substantial cognitive gaps between ethnic groups still persist, suggesting an underlying biological or evolutionary cause. They frequently hypothesize that certain alleles providing competitive advantages for foraging or navigation were favored in specific ancestral environments and became statistically linked to specific population groups, rather than identifying a single \"smoking gun\" gene for intelligence itself. However, it is crucial to note that there is no consensus in the broader scientific community on which specific biological traits drive this, and many prominent geneticists argue that the evidence for distinct genetic races corresponding to cognitive differences remains inconclusive and is heavily criticized from a population genetics standpoint.",
